   "kind": "bullets",
   "heading": "The problem",
   "bullets": [
    "Return node names of a tree-like graph level by level."
   ],
   "narration": "Here's the problem. Return node names of a tree-like graph level by level."
  },
  {
   "kind": "bullets",
   "heading": "Intuition",
   "bullets": [
    "Breadth-first search visits nodes level by level using a queue."
   ],
   "narration": "Breadth-first search visits nodes level by level using a queue."
  },
  {
   "kind": "bullets",
   "heading": "Brute force vs optimal",
   "bullets": [
    "Same O(V + E) as DFS — the order is what differs",
    "Queue (FIFO) instead of stack/recursion",
    "BFS gives shortest paths in unweighted graphs"
   ],
   "narration": "Breadth-first search is linear, like depth-first search; what changes is using a queue, so nodes come out level by level. That's why BFS finds shortest paths in unweighted graphs."
  },
  {
   "kind": "bullets",
   "heading": "Approach",
   "bullets": [
    "1. Enqueue the root",
    "2. Pop, record the name, enqueue children",
    "3. Repeat until the queue is empty"
   ],
   "narration": "Step by step. First, enqueue the root. Second, pop, record the name, enqueue children. Third, repeat until the queue is empty."
  },
