  {
   "kind": "code",
   "heading": "Java solution",
   "code": "public class BreadthFirstSearch {\n    public static class Node {\n        public final String name;\n        public final List<Node> children = new ArrayList<>();\n        public Node(String name) { this.name = name; }\n        public Node addChild(String n) { children.add(new Node(n)); return this; }\n\n        public List<String> breadthFirstSearch(List<String> out) {\n            ArrayDeque<Node> q = new ArrayDeque<>();\n            q.add(this);\n            while (!q.isEmpty()) { Node c = q.poll(); out.add(c.name); q.addAll(c.children); }\n            return out;\n        }\n    }\n}",
   "lang": "java",
   "font_size": 26,
   "filename": "BreadthFirstSearch.java",
   "narration": "We push the start node onto an array deque. While it's non-empty, we poll a node, append its name, and add all its children to the back."
  },
  {
   "kind": "bullets",
   "heading": "Complexity",
   "bullets": [
    "Time: O(v + e)",
    "Space: O(v)"
   ],
   "narration": "Time is O of v plus e. Space is O of v."
  },
  {
   "kind": "bullets",
   "heading": "Edge cases & pitfalls",
   "bullets": [
    "Use a real queue (ArrayDeque), not list.remove(0)",
    "General graphs need a visited set",
    "Children enqueued left to right"
   ],
   "narration": "Use a proper queue; removing from the front of an array list is linear. On general graphs, mark nodes visited when you enqueue them."
  },
